{
  "_comment": "The route limit covers the pre-join entrance (#712); SpaceCall loads the admitted call after Join (#730), and builds its join client from the generated ChalkPublicApi (only the unauthenticated /v1/public operations), so the MCP OAuth contract (#814) stays out of the route. The route measures about 205.5 KiB and its ceiling is 217 KiB, down from 219. Five fresh-context bundle-only runs per revision on Linux x64, 8-core Intel Xeon, Node 24.19.0, pnpm 12.8.1 and Chromium 151.0.7922.34 give non-board JavaScript gzip min/median/max of 386.486/386.486/386.486 KiB on master 35a9d072c and 386.560/386.560/386.560 KiB with send recovery. Validating the call path with Effect Schema instead of zod, which also removes zod and its English locale from the call JavaScript, cut 2.1 KiB (386.5 to 384.5 KiB on the same machine), so the non-board ceiling went down from 387 to 385 KiB. Board, CSS, timing, video-quality and leak limits and the historical timing baseline remain unchanged. The route limit alone falls, from 219 to 217 KiB. The route CSS ceiling is 28 KiB: it rose from 32 to 33 for the transcript language picker (#880) and fell to 28 in #919. Moving the SDK and UI styles onto the design-token scale (#875) turns values such as 12px into var(--space-3) inside the SDK CSS-in-JS strings, which adds 0.4 KiB gzip (385.4 KiB measured by the CI call-performance lane against 385), so the non-board ceiling goes from 385 to 386 KiB. CSS stays at 49.3 of 54 KiB. Setting the entries back to 385 undoes it. The action.* limits moved here unchanged from the latency kit's own budget file. Proposed from 40 kept cycles per plane on staging: one headless and one headed Chromium run from a 4 vCPU droplet in DigitalOcean sgp1 on 7 Nov 2026. Each p95Ms is 1.5 times the larger of the two runs' p95, rounded up to 50 ms. A limit that differs by media plane is keyed by the plane first, for example chalk_sfu.action.leave_ms; the cf_sfu ones warn until seven nightly values of that plane, from at least three nights, stay within tolerancePct. The SFU loopback and impairment tests are deterministic, so their pass rates must stay at 1 and gate from the start. proof.leave_median_ms starts as a warning at the Leave proof's own 5000 ms ceiling on the slowest leave. Real-node budgets (sfu.*, nightly action.*, proof.*, device.*) set gate to true only in a budgets pull request that cites seven nightly values, from at least three nights, with a run-to-run spread below tolerancePct. The one exception is the 18 chalk_sfu.action.* entries, which gate from 8 Nov 2026 so the own SFU has a blocking latency budget before nightly values exist: each ceiling is twice the larger p95 of the two 40-cycle runs on 7 Nov 2026 (staging, headless and headed Chromium 151, DigitalOcean sgp1 4 vCPU client, the table in #889), rounded up to 50 ms. They are loose on purpose: the 7-night rule later tightens each one to its real budget in its own pull request. New warn-only entries on 8 Nov 2026: sfu.loss_pct 0.02 is twice the worst per-run p95 loss (0.009%) of the five stall-free runs in the 7 Nov rollout data (smoke, two ramps, the 12 minute 5-class production ramp, the 1 hour 4-class soak); sfu.stalls_per_class_hour 0 because all five had none and the capacity rule is no stall over 500 ms; sfu.admission_refusals 4 is twice the worst of those runs (2, the soak). Each live run gives one value of the last two, so n is 1. proof.join_startup.* is 30000, the SDK's 30 s web startup limit, since a join over it fails instead of going live; the only measurements are the one-off runs in #526 (18.3 s for one silent dial, 11.4 to 23.3 s for 30 mixed-loss joins). No workflow scores the join proof yet. proof.leave_median_ms stays at 5000 and warn-only: the Leave proof takes five samples per run and no run values are kept. On 9 Nov 2026 chalk_sfu.action.camera_toggle.off_ms rose from 650 to 750 and chalk_sfu.action.unpublish.removed_ms from 1000 to 1100. They are interim regression ceilings measured from US-hosted runners (about 200 ms to the Singapore node): p50 636 and 947 against ceilings of 650 and 1000 made the gate depend on the runner's Azure region. They are not the accepted targets, which are much tighter, and the RTT-normalized gate replaces them. On 9 Nov 2026 the web JavaScript gzip ceilings (the 14 web.*.js_gzip_kib entries) rose from 386 to 387 KiB because the receiver's early pull from the Sync media item (#1042) adds 0.16 KiB to a call that was at 386.0; setting them back to 386 undoes it. Later on 10 Nov 2026 the same 14 entries rose from 387 to 388 KiB because the SDK code that lets the own SFU mute a camera or microphone in place (#1043) adds 0.44 KiB to a call that measured 387.4 against a master near 387.0, even while the capability is off; setting them back to 387 undoes it.",
